Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.520
This is clay, mine came from the store but&nbsp;
you could even dig it up yourself from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.680
earth where the soil contains clay minerals.&nbsp;
When you bake it, it becomes hard so you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.360
make an assortment of random objects but people&nbsp;
much more talented than me have been using it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.680
for thousands of years for things like pottery&nbsp;
and buildings, and clay was even the first known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:30.320
writing medium. I find it delightful to be able to&nbsp;
look at ancient clay tablets from the Babylonians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:38.160
4000 years ago, many of them are mathematical and&nbsp;
show an amazing level of knowledge and accuracy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.680
Scribes were taught how to record the information&nbsp;
onto the clay at a kind of scribe school and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.840
is within the relics of these schools that we&nbsp;
can find the first known homework assignments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.880
What's great is that it's actually not too&nbsp;
hard to read these tablets for ourselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.480
the numbers are marked in&nbsp;
the clay with a wedged tool.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.800
This is one,&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.880
two,
three,&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.040
four,
five,&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.280
six,
seven,&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.840
eight,
nine,&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.160
and this is ten.
So, twenty,&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.480
thirty,
forty,&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.720
fifty,
and since they're using base sixty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.160
this symbol is again 60. So it could be 60 or&nbsp;
1 depending on its place within the number.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.480
To get the hang of it let's start with the work&nbsp;
of a first-year student at scribe school where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:45.440
the writing is a lot larger and clumsier than&nbsp;
the more accomplished students. These are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:57.360
relatively easy multiplication exercises. In the&nbsp;
first row we have 50 multiplied by 45. We would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:06.560
normally work that out to be 2250 in our number&nbsp;
system but the answer written here looks like 37&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:14.880
30. we can go ahead and see that this is indeed&nbsp;
the same as what we would get, since there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:21.600
symbol for zero the place values are up for&nbsp;
some interpretation but for this one the 30s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:35.840
is essentially the ones position and 37 is the&nbsp;
60s so 37 times 60 plus 30 is equal to 2250.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.320
At school we often have to memorize the&nbsp;
multiplication tables but something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:48.720
all scribe students would have had to memorize is&nbsp;
a table of reciprocals which would make division&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:56.560
a lot easier. This is an example of one such table&nbsp;
written by a student but apparently there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:03.840
so many errors in it that an angry teacher has&nbsp;
crossed it out completely on both sides. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.480
tables would list that one-third of 60 is 20,
one-fourth of 60 is 15,&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.680
a fifth is 12,
a sixth is 10, and so on.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:24.400
60 is a great base to use because it divides into&nbsp;
lots of numbers exactly. To divide by any number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:31.840
on this table you just have to multiply by the&nbsp;
reciprocal but if your teacher is a big meanie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:39.040
they might want you to divide by a weird number&nbsp;
like 7 or 13 which 60 doesn't divide exactly into.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.120
These kind of problems are a lot harder and&nbsp;
you see an example of one of these questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:51.440
on this tiny little tablet here. It's small&nbsp;
and can fit easily in the palm of your hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:57.840
and it was likely intended for a student of scribe&nbsp;
school to take it home with them to complete and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:06.160
return to the teacher with the solution. It is one&nbsp;
of our earliest ever examples of homework. It asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:25.520
the student to show that 1 1 1 1 divided by 13 is&nbsp;
4 41 37. For us that number is 1 plus 60, plus 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:33.360
squared, plus 60 cubed which is two hundred and&nbsp;
nineteen thousand six hundred and sixty one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:44.880
That we will want to divide by thirteen and for&nbsp;
us doing it on a calculator that gives 16 897.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:54.800
The answer shown on the tablet converted&nbsp;
to our number system is 37 plus 41 times 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:03.360
plus four times 60 squared which is the same&nbsp;
sixteen thousand eight hundred and ninety seven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:11.840
The babylonian students would likely have had&nbsp;
to solve this by doing 60 cubed, 60 squared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:19.200
60, and 1 all individually divided by 13 and&nbsp;
adding up the results and the remainders.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.040
I just find it amazing that not only were&nbsp;
they doing pretty hard math problems but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.320
the way they recorded their working and answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:34.800
are so simple that we can actually understand&nbsp;
and replicate them thousands of years later.

