00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.960
What got me interested in babylonian math again&nbsp;
recently was a paper that was published a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:08.880
weeks ago by an Australian mathematician who&nbsp;
had discovered the oldest known example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:17.600
applied geometry. The tablet Si.427 shows land&nbsp;
boundaries and was drawn by a scribe who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:23.840
have graduated school and gone out into the&nbsp;
field. Practical issues of land ownership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:31.040
sales and taxes is after all exactly what a scribe&nbsp;
would have trained for. The land in question is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:38.720
split into very precise shapes but to make truly&nbsp;
perpendicular lines was not trivial and something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:45.600
that is pretty special about this tablet is that&nbsp;
it shows the Babylonians using Pythagorean triples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:51.120
in order to ensure that the perpendicular&nbsp;
lines do indeed meet at right angles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.880
It is Pythagoras's theorem that says in a right&nbsp;
angle triangle a squared plus b squared is equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:06.320
to c squared and a Pythagorean triple is three&nbsp;
positive integers that meet this condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:13.520
such as three four and five. This tablet&nbsp;
was made at least a thousand years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:22.080
Pythagoras was even born but the concept was also&nbsp;
used in India and China before then so Pythagoras&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:29.040
was hardly the first person to invent the idea&nbsp;
anyway. Here you can see a triangle with sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:38.480
seven-thirty, eighteen, and nineteen-thirty,&nbsp;
for us that's seven and a half, eighteen, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:48.400
nineteen and a half which is a scaled Pythagorean&nbsp;
triple. It's just 1.5 times the triple 5, 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:56.960
13. This rectangle here has the same dimensions&nbsp;
and was probably used to keep these sides in equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:04.000
length which could then be extended to make the&nbsp;
rest of the boundaries. This discovery of how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:11.360
used Pythagorean triples was only published a few&nbsp;
weeks ago but we had already known that they knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:19.600
trigonometry from another Babylonian tablet&nbsp;
Plimpton 322, a list of Pythagorean triples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:26.960
together with the information needed to resize&nbsp;
them. This list likely belonged to a teacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:33.920
maybe at one of the scribe schools and served&nbsp;
as a powerful reference. The fact we are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:41.040
learning so much about this ancient civilization&nbsp;
and how they did mathematics is really exciting.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:47.120
Here are a few more fascinating homework problems;&nbsp;
this palm-sized tablet shows a circle drawn inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:53.280
a square and we believe the question for the&nbsp;
student was to find the side length of the square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.920
given the shortest distance from the circle to&nbsp;
the square and the area of the region between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:08.080
circle and the square. If we label that area B,&nbsp;
the distance d, which is written on there as 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:15.200
the side length of the square as S, and r as&nbsp;
the radius of the circle, then we have these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:22.320
two equations. That the area of the square&nbsp;
minus the area of the circle is B and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:28.800
two times the distance plus two times the radius&nbsp;
of the circle is the side length of the square.&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.080
We can eliminate the variable r and then&nbsp;
doing a bit of work to get a quadratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:41.280
that can be solved for S. For this problem the&nbsp;
Babylonian approximation of pi was 3 and we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:50.320
been able to figure out that the value of B was&nbsp;
likely given as what we would call 2376.5625

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:56.400
The quadratic was one that seemed fairly&nbsp;
tricky so I used WolframAlpha to solve it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:03.440
and that gave me the answer of 52 and a half which&nbsp;
matches up with what you'll see written on the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:10.800
left of the tablet it says 52 30 and on the&nbsp;
tablet you'll also find that value squared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:17.680
which is the total area of the square. Isn't&nbsp;
it amazing that this slab of such ancient clay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:24.800
can help us here in 2021 to sharpen our&nbsp;
math skills even without understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.720
any of the historical or cultural&nbsp;
context of when it was written.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:38.240
Another amazing little tablet is this one here,&nbsp;
YBC 7289 contains an approximation for the square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:45.040
root of two which is accurate to six decimal&nbsp;
places and that's the greatest known computational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:51.280
accuracy found anywhere in the ancient world.&nbsp;
The square root of two is an infamous number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:57.440
it's irrational, it goes on forever and can't&nbsp;
be written as the ratio of two integers. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:04.480
rumour goes that Pythagoras and his squad were so&nbsp;
shocked by this concept that they drowned a guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:11.680
at sea for so much as suggesting it but here&nbsp;
are the Babylonians a thousand years earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:18.880
assigning it for homework. They have a square with&nbsp;
side length one and that makes a triangle where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:25.600
can use what we call Pythagoras's theorem to find&nbsp;
the hypotenuse squared by adding up the squares of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:33.920
both the sides in this case the hypotenuse squared&nbsp;
is one plus one so the hypotenuse is the square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:47.200
root of two. The number given here on the tablet&nbsp;
is 1 24 51 10 which as a decimal is 1.414213

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:54.560
So now we know the ratio of the diagonal&nbsp;
to the side of any square and can scale it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:01.280
On the tablet one side is labelled 30 and say&nbsp;
for example we wanted to find the diagonal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:07.200
a square with side length 30 we would&nbsp;
just do 30 times the square root of 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:17.280
and doing that gives us the last number here&nbsp;
on the tablet 42 25 35 which is 42.426 for us.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:24.240
So I guess maybe it's worth taking some care with&nbsp;
your homework assignments just in case they get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:30.880
studied by historians in the future and used to&nbsp;
make judgments on how advanced your entire society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:38.080
was. Apparently only half the tablets still in&nbsp;
existence have been translated so I'm sure we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:44.800
will find plenty more interesting stories within&nbsp;
them and if you want to discover something new for&nbsp;&nbsp;

